//! member: true
//!
//! std.x.tls.cert — X.509 v3 certificate parsing and Ed25519 chain
//! validation (RFC 5280 over X.690 DER). Stability tier: x — the THIRD
//! rung of the stdc-tls campaign (sprint sc22), the rung that makes
//! sc21's opaque Certificate bytes MEAN something: sc21 carries the DER
//! and builds the §4.4.3 signed octets; this module parses the
//! certificate, checks its signature with the issuer's Ed25519 key
//! (sc18), and validates a chain against a trust anchor, a hostname and
//! a SUPPLIED time.
//!
//! **The nursery banner (D31).** `std.x.tls.cert` is not `std.tls`: its
//! path, names and behaviour may change or vanish in one sprint; no
//! other std module may depend on it. Its reason and graduation trigger
//! live in `std/x/README.md`. It depends on ONE sibling resident —
//! `std.x.crypto.curve25519` (Ed25519 verify) — a resident-on-resident
//! edge the graduation move to `std.tls`/`std.crypto` must resolve,
//! named in the register beside the record/handshake/jose edges.
//!
//! **Built from the DOCUMENTS, not ported.** ITU-T X.690 (the DER
//! encoding rules — §8.1 TLV structure and lengths, §8.3 INTEGER
//! minimality, §8.19 OID subidentifiers, §10 the definite-form and
//! minimal-length rules, §11.1 BOOLEAN, §11.5 DEFAULT-value absence,
//! §11.6 SET-OF ordering) and RFC 5280 (the X.509/PKIX profile — §4.1
//! the certificate structure, §4.1.2.2 serial numbers, §4.1.2.5 the
//! UTCTime/GeneralizedTime split and the 2-digit-year window, §4.2 the
//! extension rules, §6.1 path validation) plus RFC 8410 (Ed25519
//! AlgorithmIdentifier: NO parameters, §3). Section numbers are cited
//! at the rejection sites they license.
//!
//! ## The robustness contract (the sprint's flagship)
//!
//! Every length read from the input is VALIDATED against the remaining
//! window before a single content byte is touched: a length that claims
//! more bytes than remain, an indefinite length (X.690 §10.1 forbids it
//! in DER), a non-minimal length encoding, a reserved length octet —
//! each is a clean, named `der` rejection, never an out-of-bounds read
//! and never a trap. wolf's checked arithmetic (X3) and region memory
//! make the overrun STRUCTURALLY impossible; the decoder's job on top
//! of that is to turn "impossible" into "rejected", because a server
//! cannot abort on a bad input. The decoder is grammar-directed (it
//! walks exactly the X.509 productions, and carries anything it does
//! not model as opaque bytes), so there is NO unbounded recursion for a
//! nesting bomb to exhaust: a deeply nested structure inside an unknown
//! extension is never walked at all.
//!
//! ## The scope line (honest about the crypto ladder)
//!
//! Signature VALIDATION is Ed25519 only (sc18's `curve25519.verify`) —
//! the D53 ladder has Ed25519/X25519, not RSA or ECDSA-P256. A
//! certificate signed with any other algorithm PARSES structurally
//! (the hardening win is algorithm-independent) and validation answers
//! the named `unsupported_alg` rejection — NEVER a silent accept: an
//! unverified signature reported as valid would be a correctness
//! failure, and that honesty is this rung's load-bearing post. RSA and
//! ECDSA validation arrive with their crypto rungs. NAMED RESIDUE
//! (deliberately not half-built): revocation (CRL/OCSP — its own rung),
//! RFC 6125 wildcard matching (`host_matches` is exact-label only),
//! the CN-fallback for hostnames (deprecated; SAN is the only source
//! here), name constraints and the policy machinery (RFC 5280 §4.2.1.10
//! / §6.1's policy tree), and non-dNSName SAN forms (IP, URI, email are
//! carried, not matched).
//!
//! ## ct: certificates are PUBLIC data
//!
//! Everything this module touches — the DER, the names, the validity
//! dates, the public keys, the signatures — is public wire material.
//! No secret is present anywhere in this module, so no function here
//! carries a constant-time obligation (unlike the handshake's secrets
//! or sc18's signing scalar; the sc18 rule: VERIFY is public data).
//! Every `pub` fn states `ct: public data` so the posture is explicit
//! rather than inferred. The 0..255 ingestion guard is the recorded
//! value-dependent branch shared with the whole ladder (F-0035's real
//! byte type retires it); it runs on public bytes here.
//!
//! ## Time is a SUPPLIED argument, never the wall clock
//!
//! Validity checking takes `now` as an argument in the compact form
//! `yyyymmddhhmmss` (UTC, the `timestamp` helper builds it). Certificate
//! times decode to the same form, so the comparison is plain integer
//! order and every test is deterministic — nothing in this module reads
//! a clock (it is capability-free: pure, comptime-refused only by its
//! delegate curve25519 when a validation reaches a signature).
//!
//! ## The ws05-consumable seam (FROZEN)
//!
//! `validate_chain(chain, hostname, anchor, now) -> Cert ! {...}` is
//! the seam wolf-wws ws05 consumes: `chain` is the peer's certificate
//! chain as CONCATENATED DER, leaf first (exactly the `cert_data`
//! entries of sc21's Certificate message, joined — DER is
//! self-delimiting, `cert_end` finds each boundary); `hostname` is the
//! SNI/target name; `anchor` is the trust anchor's DER (the pinned
//! Ed25519 CA); `now` is the supplied validation time. The answer is
//! the parsed LEAF (subject, SAN, validity, SPKI — the fields ws05
//! reads) or a NAMED rejection: `der` (malformed encoding),
//! `unsupported_alg`, `bad_signature`, `expired`, `not_yet_valid`,
//! `name_mismatch`, `not_ca`, `issuer_mismatch`, `unknown_critical`.
//! sc22 owns certificate meaning; sc21 owns the handshake flow that
//! carries the bytes; ws05 owns the socket conversation. The seam is
//! frozen at sc22; `std/x/README.md` records it.
//!
//! Byte sequences are `List[int]` with the 0..255 element contract
//! (§11's interim, F-0035), the whole ladder's convention. Chain
//! validation is iterative (no recursion, no per-chain allocation
//! beyond the parsed certificates); the trust anchor is validated like
//! any chain certificate (validity + extension discipline) — a
//! deliberately STRICTER reading than RFC 5280 §6.1.1(d)'s
//! anchor-as-input, stated here because the pinned local CA is a
//! certificate this module can and does hold to the profile.

use std.x.crypto.curve25519

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Byte helpers — the 0..255 contract, sub-slices, equality. All public
// data; the helpers are O(n) walks (the portable List shapes, F-0078).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The 0..255 ingestion guard (§11's `List[int]` byte contract).
/// ct: NOT free of value-dependent branches — the recorded ct exception
/// shared verbatim with the ladder (it leaves with F-0035's real byte
/// type). Runs only on public certificate bytes here.
fn require_byte(v: int) {
    if v < 0 {
        assert(false, "tls.cert: element outside 0..255")
    }
    if v > 255 {
        assert(false, "tls.cert: element outside 0..255")
    }
}

/// The bytes of `b` from offset `from` (inclusive) to `to` (exclusive),
/// as a fresh list. Offsets are the module's own (computed from
/// validated lengths), so a violation would be a bug in std, not in the
/// caller's data — hence the trap-on-contract posture via the checked
/// walk (nothing here can actually go out of bounds: the walk is
/// bounded by the list itself).
fn sub(b: List[int], from: int, to: int) -> List[int] {
    var out = List[int]()
    var i = 0
    for v in b {
        if i >= from {
            if i < to {
                require_byte(v)
                (mut out).push(v)
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    out
}

/// Byte-list equality. Public data; no constant-time obligation (names
/// and encodings are public — the sc18 verify-is-public rule).
fn bytes_eq(a: List[int], b: List[int]) -> bool {
    if a.len != b.len {
        return false
    }
    var i = 0
    var same = true
    while i < a.len {
        if a[i] != b[i] {
            same = false
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    same
}

/// X.690 §11.6 SET-OF order: `a <= b` comparing encodings as octet
/// strings, the shorter padded at its trailing end with zero octets.
fn set_le(a: List[int], b: List[int]) -> bool {
    var n = a.len
    if b.len > n {
        n = b.len
    }
    var i = 0
    while i < n {
        var x = 0
        if i < a.len {
            x = a[i]
        }
        var y = 0
        if i < b.len {
            y = b[i]
        }
        if x < y {
            return true
        }
        if x > y {
            return false
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    true
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// The DER reader (X.690 §8.1/§10). A cursor over the input with a hard
// window: `pos` may never pass `end`, and every composite narrows `end`
// to its own content bound before its children parse. Every rejection
// is the `der` mark — one tag, because a caller acts the same way on
// every malformed encoding (§12's one-tag-per-actionable-mode rule);
// WHICH clause failed is a diagnostic concern, and the conformance
// tests pin each clause by construction.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

struct Rd {
    b: List[int],
    pos: int,
    end: int,
}

struct Tlv {
    tag: int,
    cons: bool,
    len: int,
}

/// One byte, advancing. Truncation (reading past the window) is the
/// canonical `der` rejection — never an out-of-bounds read.
fn rd_byte(mut r: Rd) -> int ! {der} {
    if r.pos >= r.end {
        return der
    }
    let v = r.b[r.pos]
    if v < 0 {
        return der
    }
    if v > 255 {
        return der
    }
    r.pos = r.pos + 1
    v
}

/// The TLV header at the cursor: identifier octet + definite length,
/// both validated (X.690 §8.1). Rejects: a multi-byte tag number (0x1F
/// low bits — X.509 uses none, §8.1.2.4 scoped out by profile), the
/// indefinite length 0x80 (§10.1: DER is definite-form only), the
/// reserved octet 0xFF (§8.1.3.5(c)), a length-of-length above 4 (no
/// certificate approaches 2^31; anything larger cannot fit the window
/// either), a non-minimal length (§10.1: the shortest form is
/// mandatory — a long form whose value fits the short form, or a
/// leading zero length octet), and a length exceeding the bytes that
/// REMAIN in the window (the overrun class).
fn rd_tag_len(mut r: Rd) -> Tlv ! {der} {
    let t = rd_byte(mut r)?
    if t % 32 == 31 {
        return der
    }
    let cons = t / 32 % 2 == 1
    let first = rd_byte(mut r)?
    var length = 0
    if first < 128 {
        length = first
    } else {
        if first == 128 {
            return der
        }
        if first == 255 {
            return der
        }
        let n = first - 128
        if n > 4 {
            return der
        }
        var acc = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let d = rd_byte(mut r)?
            if i == 0 {
                if d == 0 {
                    return der
                }
            }
            acc = acc * 256 + d
            i = i + 1
        }
        if acc < 128 {
            return der
        }
        length = acc
    }
    if length > r.end - r.pos {
        return der
    }
    Tlv { tag: t, cons: cons, len: length }
}

/// The TLV header, requiring the exact identifier octet `tag` (which
/// encodes class, constructed bit and number at once, so a primitive
/// SEQUENCE or a constructed INTEGER is rejected by the same equality).
/// Returns the content length.
fn rd_expect(mut r: Rd, tag: int) -> int ! {der} {
    let t = rd_tag_len(mut r)?
    if t.tag != tag {
        return der
    }
    t.len
}

/// Skip `n` content bytes (already validated against the window by the
/// header read that produced `n`).
fn rd_skip(mut r: Rd, n: int) {
    r.pos = r.pos + n
}

/// The content bytes of length `n` from the cursor, advancing.
fn rd_take(mut r: Rd, n: int) -> List[int] {
    let out = sub(r.b, r.pos, r.pos + n)
    r.pos = r.pos + n
    out
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// DER value parsers — INTEGER, BOOLEAN, OID, BIT STRING, the string
// types, the two Time types. Each enforces the DER clause it cites.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// INTEGER content minimality (X.690 §8.3.2): at least one octet; a
/// leading 0x00 is legal only before a high bit, a leading 0xFF only
/// before a clear one.
fn int_minimal(bs: List[int]) -> bool {
    if bs.len == 0 {
        return false
    }
    if bs.len == 1 {
        return true
    }
    let b0 = bs[0]
    let b1 = bs[1]
    if b0 == 0 {
        if b1 < 128 {
            return false
        }
    }
    if b0 == 255 {
        if b1 >= 128 {
            return false
        }
    }
    true
}

/// A small non-negative INTEGER as an `int` (the version field, the
/// pathLenConstraint): content minimal, non-negative, at most 4 octets.
fn rd_small_int(mut r: Rd) -> int ! {der} {
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 2)?
    if n > 4 {
        return der
    }
    let bs = rd_take(mut r, n)
    if !int_minimal(bs) {
        return der
    }
    if bs[0] >= 128 {
        return der
    }
    var acc = 0
    for v in bs {
        acc = acc * 256 + v
    }
    acc
}

/// BOOLEAN (X.690 §8.2 + §11.1): one octet, and DER restricts TRUE to
/// 0xFF exactly — 0x01 is BER and rejected.
fn rd_boolean(mut r: Rd) -> bool ! {der} {
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 1)?
    if n != 1 {
        return der
    }
    let v = rd_byte(mut r)?
    if v == 0 {
        return false
    }
    if v == 255 {
        return true
    }
    der
}

/// OBJECT IDENTIFIER content to dotted-decimal (X.690 §8.19): non-empty,
/// each subidentifier base-128 with no 0x80 padding octet (§8.19.2's
/// minimality), the final octet's continuation bit clear, and each arc
/// capped well under `int` so the checked arithmetic can never trap on
/// adversarial input — an oversized arc is a `der` rejection instead.
fn rd_oid(mut r: Rd) -> str ! {der} {
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 6)?
    if n == 0 {
        return der
    }
    let bs = rd_take(mut r, n)
    var out = ""
    var acc = 0
    var in_sub = false
    var first_done = false
    var i = 0
    while i < bs.len {
        let v = bs[i]
        if !in_sub {
            if v == 128 {
                return der
            }
        }
        if acc > 16777215 {
            return der
        }
        acc = acc * 128 + v % 128
        in_sub = v >= 128
        if !in_sub {
            if first_done {
                out = "{out}.{acc}"
            } else {
                var root = 0
                var second = acc
                if acc >= 40 {
                    root = 1
                    second = acc - 40
                }
                if acc >= 80 {
                    root = 2
                    second = acc - 80
                }
                out = "{root}.{second}"
                first_done = true
            }
            acc = 0
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if in_sub {
        return der
    }
    out
}

/// PrintableString charset (X.680 §41.4): letters, digits, and the
/// eleven permitted marks. Anything else rejects.
fn printable_ok(bs: List[int]) -> bool {
    var ok = true
    for v in bs {
        var this_ok = false
        if v >= 65 {
            if v <= 90 {
                this_ok = true
            }
        }
        if v >= 97 {
            if v <= 122 {
                this_ok = true
            }
        }
        if v >= 48 {
            if v <= 57 {
                this_ok = true
            }
        }
        if v == 32 {
            this_ok = true
        }
        if v == 39 {
            this_ok = true
        }
        if v >= 40 {
            if v <= 41 {
                this_ok = true
            }
        }
        if v >= 43 {
            if v <= 47 {
                this_ok = true
            }
        }
        if v == 58 {
            this_ok = true
        }
        if v == 61 {
            this_ok = true
        }
        if v == 63 {
            this_ok = true
        }
        if !this_ok {
            ok = false
        }
    }
    ok
}

/// IA5String content: bytes 0..127 (ASCII), and for the names this
/// module reads (dNSName) additionally non-NUL.
fn ia5_ok(bs: List[int]) -> bool {
    var ok = true
    for v in bs {
        if v > 127 {
            ok = false
        }
        if v == 0 {
            ok = false
        }
    }
    ok
}

/// Two ASCII digits from `bs` at `i` as an int, or the miss.
fn two_digits(bs: List[int], i: int) -> int ! {der} {
    let a = bs[i]
    let b = bs[i + 1]
    if a < 48 {
        return der
    }
    if a > 57 {
        return der
    }
    if b < 48 {
        return der
    }
    if b > 57 {
        return der
    }
    (a - 48) * 10 + (b - 48)
}

/// Assemble and range-check a `yyyymmddhhmmss` timestamp from parsed
/// fields (month 1..12, day 1..31, hour 0..23, minute/second 0..59 —
/// the ranges RFC 5280's profile of ISO 8601 admits; leap seconds are
/// not represented, per the UTCTime/GeneralizedTime DER restriction to
/// second 0..59 this profile enforces).
fn build_ts(y: int, mo: int, d: int, h: int, mi: int, s: int) -> int ! {der} {
    if mo < 1 {
        return der
    }
    if mo > 12 {
        return der
    }
    if d < 1 {
        return der
    }
    if d > 31 {
        return der
    }
    if h > 23 {
        return der
    }
    if mi > 59 {
        return der
    }
    if s > 59 {
        return der
    }
    let scale: int = 10000000000
    let ymd = y * scale
    ymd + mo * 100000000 + d * 1000000 + h * 10000 + mi * 100 + s
}

/// One RFC 5280 Time (§4.1.2.5): UTCTime `YYMMDDHHMMSSZ` (seconds
/// mandatory, Z mandatory, §4.1.2.5.1; the 2-digit-year window: 50..99
/// is 19xx, 00..49 is 20xx) or GeneralizedTime `YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ`
/// (§4.1.2.5.2: no fractional seconds, Z mandatory) — and the profile's
/// split is enforced BOTH ways: dates through 2049 MUST be UTCTime, so
/// a GeneralizedTime year before 2050 is a `der` rejection (§4.1.2.5).
fn rd_time(mut r: Rd) -> int ! {der} {
    let t = rd_tag_len(mut r)?
    if t.tag == 23 {
        if t.len != 13 {
            return der
        }
        let bs = rd_take(mut r, 13)
        if bs[12] != 90 {
            return der
        }
        let yy = two_digits(bs, 0)?
        var y = 2000 + yy
        if yy >= 50 {
            y = 1900 + yy
        }
        let mo = two_digits(bs, 2)?
        let d = two_digits(bs, 4)?
        let h = two_digits(bs, 6)?
        let mi = two_digits(bs, 8)?
        let s = two_digits(bs, 10)?
        return build_ts(y, mo, d, h, mi, s)
    }
    if t.tag == 24 {
        if t.len != 15 {
            return der
        }
        let bs = rd_take(mut r, 15)
        if bs[14] != 90 {
            return der
        }
        let hi = two_digits(bs, 0)?
        let lo = two_digits(bs, 2)?
        let y = hi * 100 + lo
        if y < 2050 {
            return der
        }
        let mo = two_digits(bs, 4)?
        let d = two_digits(bs, 6)?
        let h = two_digits(bs, 8)?
        let mi = two_digits(bs, 10)?
        let s = two_digits(bs, 12)?
        return build_ts(y, mo, d, h, mi, s)
    }
    der
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// X.509 structure parsers (RFC 5280 §4.1) — AlgorithmIdentifier, Name,
// SubjectPublicKeyInfo, the extensions.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

struct Alg {
    dotted: str,
    raw: List[int],
}

struct NameInfo {
    raw: List[int],
    cn: str,
}

struct Spki {
    alg: str,
    key: List[int],
}

struct Exts {
    has_bc: bool,
    is_ca: bool,
    path_len: int,
    key_usage: int,
    has_san: bool,
    san: List[str],
    crit_unknown: str,
}

/// AlgorithmIdentifier: SEQUENCE { algorithm OID, parameters ANY
/// OPTIONAL }. The raw encoding is captured for the §4.1.2.3 equality
/// check (TBS `signature` must equal the outer `signatureAlgorithm`).
/// RFC 8410 §3: the Ed25519 identifier MUST have absent parameters —
/// present parameters (even NULL) reject. Other algorithms' parameters
/// are carried opaquely (RSA's NULL, ECDSA's named curve).
fn rd_alg(mut r: Rd) -> Alg ! {der} {
    let start = r.pos
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    let stop = r.pos + n
    let saved = r.end
    r.end = stop
    let dotted = rd_oid(mut r)?
    if dotted == "1.3.101.112" {
        if r.pos != stop {
            return der
        }
    }
    if r.pos < stop {
        let p = rd_tag_len(mut r)?
        rd_skip(mut r, p.len)
    }
    if r.pos != stop {
        return der
    }
    r.end = saved
    let raw = sub(r.b, start, stop)
    Alg { dotted: dotted, raw: raw }
}

/// One AttributeTypeAndValue: SEQUENCE { type OID, value ANY }. The CN
/// (2.5.4.3) value must be UTF8String or PrintableString (RFC 5280
/// §4.1.2.4's profile for new certificates), validated and returned;
/// every other attribute's value is carried opaquely (any single TLV).
fn rd_atv(mut r: Rd) -> str ! {der} {
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    let stop = r.pos + n
    let saved = r.end
    r.end = stop
    let attr = rd_oid(mut r)?
    let vt = rd_tag_len(mut r)?
    var cn = ""
    if attr == "2.5.4.3" {
        if vt.tag == 12 {
            let bs = rd_take(mut r, vt.len)
            let s = str_from_utf8(bs) else |_| {
                return der
            }
            cn = s
        } else {
            if vt.tag != 19 {
                return der
            }
            let bs = rd_take(mut r, vt.len)
            if !printable_ok(bs) {
                return der
            }
            let s = str_from_utf8(bs) else |_| {
                return der
            }
            cn = s
        }
    } else {
        rd_skip(mut r, vt.len)
    }
    if r.pos != stop {
        return der
    }
    r.end = saved
    cn
}

/// A Name (RFC 5280 §4.1.2.4): SEQUENCE OF RelativeDistinguishedName,
/// each a SET OF AttributeTypeAndValue with at least one member, the
/// members in X.690 §11.6 DER order (an unsorted SET rejects). The raw
/// Name encoding is captured — name chaining (§6.1's issuer/subject
/// linkage) compares these bytes exactly, the reading DER's canonical
/// encodings license (the §7.1 caseIgnoreMatch machinery is named
/// residue with the rest of the internationalized-name work).
fn rd_name(mut r: Rd) -> NameInfo ! {der} {
    let start = r.pos
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    let stop = r.pos + n
    let saved = r.end
    r.end = stop
    var cn = ""
    while r.pos < stop {
        let rdn_len = rd_expect(mut r, 49)?
        if rdn_len == 0 {
            return der
        }
        let rdn_stop = r.pos + rdn_len
        let rdn_saved = r.end
        r.end = rdn_stop
        var prev = List[int]()
        var have_prev = false
        while r.pos < rdn_stop {
            let atv_start = r.pos
            let got = rd_atv(mut r)?
            if got != "" {
                cn = got
            }
            let enc = sub(r.b, atv_start, r.pos)
            if have_prev {
                if !set_le(prev, enc) {
                    return der
                }
            }
            prev = enc
            have_prev = true
        }
        r.end = rdn_saved
    }
    if r.pos != stop {
        return der
    }
    r.end = saved
    let raw = sub(r.b, start, stop)
    NameInfo { raw: raw, cn: cn }
}

/// SubjectPublicKeyInfo (§4.1.2.7): SEQUENCE { AlgorithmIdentifier,
/// subjectPublicKey BIT STRING }. The BIT STRING must have zero unused
/// bits (every SPKI this profile reads is whole octets), and an
/// Ed25519 key must be exactly 32 bytes (RFC 8410 §4).
fn rd_spki(mut r: Rd) -> Spki ! {der} {
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    let stop = r.pos + n
    let saved = r.end
    r.end = stop
    let alg = rd_alg(mut r)?
    let bn = rd_expect(mut r, 3)?
    if bn == 0 {
        return der
    }
    let unused = rd_byte(mut r)?
    if unused != 0 {
        return der
    }
    let key = rd_take(mut r, bn - 1)
    if alg.dotted == "1.3.101.112" {
        if key.len != 32 {
            return der
        }
    }
    if r.pos != stop {
        return der
    }
    r.end = saved
    Spki { alg: alg.dotted, key: key }
}

/// basicConstraints (§4.2.1.9): SEQUENCE { cA BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
/// pathLenConstraint INTEGER OPTIONAL }. DER's §11.5 rule makes an
/// encoded FALSE a rejection (the default is expressed by ABSENCE), and
/// the RFC forbids pathLenConstraint without cA TRUE.
fn parse_bc(bs: List[int]) -> Exts ! {der} {
    let bn0 = bs.len
    var r = Rd { b: bs, pos: 0, end: bn0 }
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    let stop = r.pos + n
    var is_ca = false
    var path_len = 0 - 1
    if r.pos < stop {
        let t = rd_tag_len(mut r)?
        if t.tag == 1 {
            if t.len != 1 {
                return der
            }
            let v = rd_byte(mut r)?
            if v == 0 {
                return der
            }
            if v != 255 {
                return der
            }
            is_ca = true
            if r.pos < stop {
                path_len = rd_small_int(mut r)?
            }
        } else {
            if t.tag != 2 {
                return der
            }
            return der
        }
    }
    if r.pos != stop {
        return der
    }
    if r.pos != bn0 {
        return der
    }
    Exts {
        has_bc: true,
        is_ca: is_ca,
        path_len: path_len,
        key_usage: 0 - 1,
        has_san: false,
        san: List[str](),
        crit_unknown: "",
    }
}

/// keyUsage (§4.2.1.3): a named-bit BIT STRING, at least one bit set,
/// DER-trimmed (no trailing zero content octet, declared-unused bits
/// zero). Decoded as an int where KeyUsage bit i contributes 2^i
/// (digitalSignature = 1, keyCertSign = 32, cRLSign = 64, ...).
fn parse_ku(bs: List[int]) -> int ! {der} {
    let bn0 = bs.len
    var r = Rd { b: bs, pos: 0, end: bn0 }
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 3)?
    if n < 2 {
        return der
    }
    let unused = rd_byte(mut r)?
    if unused > 7 {
        return der
    }
    let content = rd_take(mut r, n - 1)
    if r.pos != bn0 {
        return der
    }
    let last = content[content.len - 1]
    if last == 0 {
        return der
    }
    var mask = 1
    var i = 0
    while i < unused {
        mask = mask * 2
        i = i + 1
    }
    if last % mask != 0 {
        return der
    }
    var flags = 0
    var j = 0
    for byte_v in content {
        var bit = 0
        var rest = byte_v
        var place = 128
        while bit < 8 {
            var weight = 1
            var k = 0
            while k < j * 8 + bit {
                weight = weight * 2
                k = k + 1
            }
            if rest >= place {
                rest = rest - place
                flags = flags + weight
            }
            place = place / 2
            bit = bit + 1
        }
        j = j + 1
    }
    if flags == 0 {
        return der
    }
    flags
}

/// subjectAltName (§4.2.1.6): SEQUENCE OF GeneralName with at least one
/// name. dNSName entries ([2] IA5String, ASCII, non-empty) are decoded;
/// the other GeneralName forms (otherName, rfc822Name, dirName,
/// uniformResourceIdentifier, iPAddress, registeredID, ...) are carried
/// opaquely — present, unmatched (the residue line in the header).
fn parse_san(bs: List[int]) -> List[str] ! {der} {
    let bn0 = bs.len
    var r = Rd { b: bs, pos: 0, end: bn0 }
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    if n == 0 {
        return der
    }
    let stop = r.pos + n
    var out = List[str]()
    while r.pos < stop {
        let t = rd_tag_len(mut r)?
        let cls = t.tag / 64
        if cls != 2 {
            return der
        }
        let num = t.tag % 32
        if num > 8 {
            return der
        }
        if t.tag == 130 {
            if t.len == 0 {
                return der
            }
            let bs2 = rd_take(mut r, t.len)
            if !ia5_ok(bs2) {
                return der
            }
            let name = str_from_utf8(bs2) else |_| {
                return der
            }
            (mut out).push(name)
        } else {
            rd_skip(mut r, t.len)
        }
    }
    if r.pos != stop {
        return der
    }
    if r.pos != bn0 {
        return der
    }
    out
}

/// The [3] extensions block (§4.1.2.9 + §4.2): a non-empty SEQUENCE OF
/// Extension `{ extnID OID, critical BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE, extnValue
/// OCTET STRING }`. Enforced here: an explicitly encoded FALSE rejects
/// (X.690 §11.5), a duplicate extnID rejects (§4.2: at most one
/// instance of a particular extension), and the three recognized
/// extensions parse fully. An UNRECOGNIZED extension is carried when
/// non-critical and RECORDED when critical — `parse` succeeds either
/// way (the structure is sound), and validation answers the RFC's
/// mandated rejection (`unknown_critical`) so the parse/validate split
/// survives real-world certificates.
fn rd_exts(mut r: Rd) -> Exts ! {der} {
    let outer = rd_expect(mut r, 163)?
    let outer_stop = r.pos + outer
    let outer_saved = r.end
    r.end = outer_stop
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    if n == 0 {
        return der
    }
    let stop = r.pos + n
    var has_bc = false
    var is_ca = false
    var path_len = 0 - 1
    var key_usage = 0 - 1
    var has_san = false
    var san = List[str]()
    var crit_unknown = ""
    var seen = List[str]()
    while r.pos < stop {
        let en = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
        let e_stop = r.pos + en
        let e_saved = r.end
        r.end = e_stop
        let id = rd_oid(mut r)?
        var dup = false
        for s in seen {
            if s == id {
                dup = true
            }
        }
        if dup {
            return der
        }
        (mut seen).push(id)
        var critical = false
        let peek = r.pos
        let t = rd_tag_len(mut r)?
        if t.tag == 1 {
            r.pos = peek
            let c = rd_boolean(mut r)?
            if !c {
                return der
            }
            critical = true
        } else {
            r.pos = peek
        }
        let vn = rd_expect(mut r, 4)?
        let inner = rd_take(mut r, vn)
        if r.pos != e_stop {
            return der
        }
        r.end = e_saved
        if id == "2.5.29.19" {
            let bc = parse_bc(inner)?
            has_bc = true
            is_ca = bc.is_ca
            path_len = bc.path_len
        } else if id == "2.5.29.15" {
            key_usage = parse_ku(inner)?
        } else if id == "2.5.29.17" {
            san = parse_san(inner)?
            has_san = true
        } else if critical {
            if crit_unknown == "" {
                crit_unknown = id
            }
        }
    }
    if r.pos != stop {
        return der
    }
    if r.pos != outer_stop {
        return der
    }
    r.end = outer_saved
    Exts {
        has_bc: has_bc,
        is_ca: is_ca,
        path_len: path_len,
        key_usage: key_usage,
        has_san: has_san,
        san: san,
        crit_unknown: crit_unknown,
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// The certificate (RFC 5280 §4.1) — the parsed value and the parser.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A parsed X.509 certificate — the fields wolf-wws ws05 reads, plus
/// the raw material validation needs (`tbs` is the exact DER of the
/// TBSCertificate, the octets the signature is over; `issuer`/`subject`
/// are the raw Name encodings, the chaining currency). Times are
/// compact `yyyymmddhhmmss` UTC ints. `path_len` and `key_usage` are
/// -1 when their extensions are absent; `crit_unknown` is the first
/// unrecognized CRITICAL extnID ("" when none — validation rejects a
/// non-empty one by name, RFC 5280 §4.2's rule). This pin has no
/// private fields, so a forged Cert is constructible; every consumer
/// here treats the fields as data and answers with rows, never traps.
/// ct: public data throughout — a certificate carries no secret.
pub struct Cert {
    version: int,
    serial: List[int],
    sig_alg: str,
    issuer: List[int],
    issuer_cn: str,
    subject: List[int],
    subject_cn: str,
    not_before: int,
    not_after: int,
    spki_alg: str,
    spki_key: List[int],
    has_bc: bool,
    is_ca: bool,
    path_len: int,
    key_usage: int,
    has_san: bool,
    san: List[str],
    crit_unknown: str,
    tbs: List[int],
    sig: List[int],
}

/// Parse ONE DER certificate occupying the whole of `bytes` (RFC 5280
/// §4.1 over X.690 DER; trailing bytes reject). Structural validation
/// only — signatures, dates and names are DECODED here and JUDGED in
/// `validate_chain`, so a certificate signed with an algorithm outside
/// the ladder still parses (the split the header states). Every
/// malformed, truncated or non-DER encoding is the named `der`
/// rejection — no input reads out of bounds and none traps (the
/// robustness contract; the conformance suite in `tests/x/tls/cert/`
/// pins each clause). Rejections enforced here include: every length
/// class (indefinite, non-minimal, overrun, reserved), INTEGER and
/// BOOLEAN minimality, OID subidentifier form, the version field
/// (present must be v3; v1's DEFAULT must be absent, X.690 §11.5; v2
/// rejects), serial positivity and the 20-octet cap (§4.1.2.2), the
/// §4.1.2.3 TBS/outer algorithm equality, the §4.1.2.5 time rules
/// (2-digit window, mandatory seconds and Z, the 2049/2050 split),
/// DN SET-OF order (X.690 §11.6), CN string types (§4.1.2.4),
/// SPKI form (zero unused bits; RFC 8410's no-params, 32-byte Ed25519
/// key), the §4.1.2.8 unique-ID prohibition, and the §4.2 extension
/// rules (non-empty, no duplicates, DEFAULT-FALSE absence,
/// basicConstraints/keyUsage/subjectAltName inner form).
/// ct: public data; no secret is present.
pub fn parse(bytes: List[int]) -> Cert ! {der} {
    for v in bytes {
        require_byte(v)
    }
    let n0 = bytes.len
    var r = Rd { b: bytes, pos: 0, end: n0 }
    let outer = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    let outer_stop = r.pos + outer
    if outer_stop != n0 {
        return der
    }
    let tbs_start = r.pos
    let tn = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    let tbs_stop = r.pos + tn
    let saved = r.end
    r.end = tbs_stop
    var version = 1
    let peek = r.pos
    let first = rd_tag_len(mut r)?
    if first.tag == 160 {
        let v_stop = r.pos + first.len
        let v = rd_small_int(mut r)?
        if r.pos != v_stop {
            return der
        }
        if v != 2 {
            return der
        }
        version = 3
    } else {
        r.pos = peek
    }
    let sn = rd_expect(mut r, 2)?
    if sn == 0 {
        return der
    }
    if sn > 20 {
        return der
    }
    let serial = rd_take(mut r, sn)
    if !int_minimal(serial) {
        return der
    }
    if serial[0] >= 128 {
        return der
    }
    if serial.len == 1 {
        if serial[0] == 0 {
            return der
        }
    }
    let tbs_alg = rd_alg(mut r)?
    let issuer = rd_name(mut r)?
    let vn = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    let v_stop = r.pos + vn
    let not_before = rd_time(mut r)?
    let not_after = rd_time(mut r)?
    if r.pos != v_stop {
        return der
    }
    let subject = rd_name(mut r)?
    let spki = rd_spki(mut r)?
    var exts = Exts {
        has_bc: false,
        is_ca: false,
        path_len: 0 - 1,
        key_usage: 0 - 1,
        has_san: false,
        san: List[str](),
        crit_unknown: "",
    }
    if r.pos < tbs_stop {
        let peek2 = r.pos
        let nxt = rd_tag_len(mut r)?
        if nxt.tag == 129 {
            return der
        }
        if nxt.tag == 130 {
            return der
        }
        if nxt.tag != 163 {
            return der
        }
        if version != 3 {
            return der
        }
        r.pos = peek2
        exts = rd_exts(mut r)?
    }
    if r.pos != tbs_stop {
        return der
    }
    r.end = saved
    let tbs = sub(r.b, tbs_start, tbs_stop)
    let outer_alg = rd_alg(mut r)?
    if !bytes_eq(outer_alg.raw, tbs_alg.raw) {
        return der
    }
    let bn = rd_expect(mut r, 3)?
    if bn == 0 {
        return der
    }
    let unused = rd_byte(mut r)?
    if unused != 0 {
        return der
    }
    let sig = rd_take(mut r, bn - 1)
    if r.pos != outer_stop {
        return der
    }
    Cert {
        version: version,
        serial: serial,
        sig_alg: tbs_alg.dotted,
        issuer: issuer.raw,
        issuer_cn: issuer.cn,
        subject: subject.raw,
        subject_cn: subject.cn,
        not_before: not_before,
        not_after: not_after,
        spki_alg: spki.alg,
        spki_key: spki.key,
        has_bc: exts.has_bc,
        is_ca: exts.is_ca,
        path_len: exts.path_len,
        key_usage: exts.key_usage,
        has_san: exts.has_san,
        san: exts.san,
        crit_unknown: exts.crit_unknown,
        tbs: tbs,
        sig: sig,
    }
}

/// Rewind width helper for a long-form header re-read (internal; the
/// number of extra length octets a value of `len` occupied).
fn peek_rewind(pos: int, length: int) -> int {
    var extra = 0
    var v = length
    while v > 0 {
        extra = extra + 1
        v = v / 256
    }
    pos - extra
}

/// The end offset (exclusive) of the DER certificate starting at
/// `offset` in `chain` — the boundary walker for CONCATENATED DER
/// (sc21's certificate_list entries, joined). Validates the outer TLV
/// header only; `parse` validates the body. Truncation and every
/// malformed length reject `der`.
/// ct: public data.
pub fn cert_end(chain: List[int], offset: int) -> int ! {der} {
    if offset < 0 {
        return der
    }
    if offset >= chain.len {
        return der
    }
    let n0 = chain.len
    var r = Rd { b: chain, pos: offset, end: n0 }
    let n = rd_expect(mut r, 48)?
    r.pos + n
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Judgement helpers — algorithm names, hostname matching, timestamps.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The Ed25519 algorithm OID (RFC 8410): the one signature algorithm
/// this rung VALIDATES. Zero-argument functions stand in for `const`s
/// (F-0025).
/// ct: public data.
pub fn oid_ed25519() -> str {
    "1.3.101.112"
}

/// Classify a dotted algorithm OID: "ed25519" (1.3.101.112),
/// "rsa" (the PKCS#1 arc — signature algorithms and rsaEncryption),
/// "ecdsa" (the X9.62 arc — ECDSA signatures and id-ecPublicKey),
/// "ed448" (1.3.101.113), else "unknown". Classification is for
/// REPORTING; validation asks for Ed25519 exactly and answers
/// `unsupported_alg` for everything else.
/// ct: public data.
pub fn alg_name(dotted: str) -> str {
    if dotted == "1.3.101.112" {
        return "ed25519"
    }
    if dotted == "1.3.101.113" {
        return "ed448"
    }
    if dotted.starts_with("1.2.840.113549.1.1.") {
        return "rsa"
    }
    if dotted.starts_with("1.2.840.10045.") {
        return "ecdsa"
    }
    "unknown"
}

/// KeyUsage bit weight: digitalSignature (bit 0).
/// ct: public data.
pub fn ku_digital_signature() -> int {
    1
}

/// KeyUsage bit weight: keyCertSign (bit 5) — the bit an issuing CA's
/// keyUsage must assert (§4.2.1.3).
/// ct: public data.
pub fn ku_key_cert_sign() -> int {
    32
}

/// Exact, case-insensitive DNS name match (DNS names compare
/// case-insensitively; both sides are lowered). NO wildcard support:
/// RFC 6125 §6.4.3 wildcard matching is named residue in the module
/// header — a `*.` pattern here simply never matches a hostname, which
/// fails CLOSED.
/// ct: public data.
pub fn host_matches(pattern: str, host: str) -> bool {
    if pattern == "" {
        return false
    }
    if host == "" {
        return false
    }
    pattern.lower() == host.lower()
}

/// Build the compact `yyyymmddhhmmss` timestamp `validate_chain` and
/// the Cert fields speak (UTC). Caller-contract violations trap
/// `assert` (X3's posture: the caller could have checked the ranges);
/// certificate DATA with bad ranges rejects `der` in the parser
/// instead — §10's trap-where-the-caller-broke-it rule.
/// ct: public data.
pub fn timestamp(y: int, mo: int, d: int, h: int, mi: int, s: int) -> int {
    assert(y >= 0, "tls.cert: year before 0")
    assert(y <= 9999, "tls.cert: year after 9999")
    assert(mo >= 1, "tls.cert: month outside 1..12")
    assert(mo <= 12, "tls.cert: month outside 1..12")
    assert(d >= 1, "tls.cert: day outside 1..31")
    assert(d <= 31, "tls.cert: day outside 1..31")
    assert(h >= 0, "tls.cert: hour outside 0..23")
    assert(h <= 23, "tls.cert: hour outside 0..23")
    assert(mi >= 0, "tls.cert: minute outside 0..59")
    assert(mi <= 59, "tls.cert: minute outside 0..59")
    assert(s >= 0, "tls.cert: second outside 0..59")
    assert(s <= 59, "tls.cert: second outside 0..59")
    let scale: int = 10000000000
    y * scale + mo * 100000000 + d * 1000000 + h * 10000 + mi * 100 + s
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Signature validation (Ed25519, sc18) and chain validation (RFC 5280
// §6.1, scoped to what Ed25519 chains exercise).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Verify `child`'s signature over its TBSCertificate bytes with
/// `issuer`'s Ed25519 key. `unsupported_alg` when the child's signature
/// algorithm or the issuer's key algorithm is anything but Ed25519 —
/// the NAMED honest rejection, never a silent accept (the header's
/// scope line). `bad_signature` when the Ed25519 verification fails
/// (sc18's `verify` also answers false for wrong-length keys or
/// signatures and non-canonical encodings, so those are `bad_signature`
/// too). Returns 1 on success (the accept_finished shape).
/// ct: public data — an Ed25519 VERIFY handles no secret (the sc18
/// rule); curve25519's own ct: lines govern its internals.
pub fn verify_signed_by(child: Cert, issuer: Cert) -> int ! {unsupported_alg, bad_signature} {
    if child.sig_alg != oid_ed25519() {
        return unsupported_alg
    }
    if issuer.spki_alg != oid_ed25519() {
        return unsupported_alg
    }
    if !curve25519.verify(issuer.spki_key, child.tbs, child.sig) {
        return bad_signature
    }
    1
}

/// The per-certificate judgements shared by every chain position:
/// an unrecognized CRITICAL extension rejects by name (RFC 5280 §4.2's
/// MUST), and the validity window is checked against the SUPPLIED
/// `now` (§4.1.2.5, §6.1.3(a)(2)) — `not_yet_valid` before the window,
/// `expired` after it.
fn check_cert_at(c: Cert, now: int) -> int ! {expired, not_yet_valid, unknown_critical} {
    if c.crit_unknown != "" {
        return unknown_critical
    }
    if now < c.not_before {
        return not_yet_valid
    }
    if now > c.not_after {
        return expired
    }
    1
}

/// The issuing-capability judgement (§6.1.4(k) + §4.2.1.3 + §4.2.1.9):
/// an issuer must carry basicConstraints with cA TRUE; a present
/// keyUsage must assert keyCertSign; and a present pathLenConstraint
/// must admit `below` intermediate certificates beneath this issuer.
/// All three failures are the `not_ca` rejection — "this certificate
/// may not sign at this position", which is the one thing a caller can
/// act on.
fn check_issuer_at(c: Cert, below: int) -> int ! {not_ca} {
    if !c.has_bc {
        return not_ca
    }
    if !c.is_ca {
        return not_ca
    }
    if c.key_usage >= 0 {
        if c.key_usage / 32 % 2 != 1 {
            return not_ca
        }
    }
    if c.path_len >= 0 {
        if below > c.path_len {
            return not_ca
        }
    }
    1
}

/// The leaf's hostname judgement: the target `hostname` must match a
/// dNSName in the leaf's subjectAltName (exact, case-insensitive —
/// `host_matches`). A leaf with NO SAN never matches (the CN fallback
/// is deprecated and deliberately not implemented — named residue).
fn check_hostname(c: Cert, hostname: str) -> int ! {name_mismatch} {
    if !c.has_san {
        return name_mismatch
    }
    var matched = false
    for n in c.san {
        if host_matches(n, hostname) {
            matched = true
        }
    }
    if !matched {
        return name_mismatch
    }
    1
}

/// THE ws05 SEAM (frozen — the module header's contract): validate the
/// peer's certificate chain against a trust anchor, a target hostname
/// and a SUPPLIED time, returning the parsed LEAF on success.
///
/// `chain` is concatenated DER, LEAF FIRST (sc21's certificate_list
/// cert_data entries joined; a single leaf is the common case).
/// `anchor` is the trust anchor's DER (the pinned Ed25519 CA) — the
/// chain may or may not include it; a self-signed tail chains to it
/// either way. `now` is `timestamp(...)`-shaped UTC.
///
/// Checks, in order, each a NAMED rejection (RFC 5280 §6.1 scoped to
/// what Ed25519 chains exercise): the encodings (`der`), every
/// certificate's critical-extension discipline (`unknown_critical`) and
/// validity window against `now` (`not_yet_valid`/`expired`), the
/// leaf's SAN against `hostname` (`name_mismatch`), each link's
/// issuer/subject name chaining by exact DER Name bytes
/// (`issuer_mismatch`), each issuer's authority — basicConstraints cA,
/// keyCertSign, pathLenConstraint (`not_ca`) — and each link's Ed25519
/// signature (`bad_signature`; `unsupported_alg` for any signature or
/// key algorithm outside the ladder, the honest scope line). The
/// cheap structural checks run BEFORE any signature so a chain that
/// fails on names or dates never pays a scalar multiplication.
/// Revocation (CRL/OCSP) is NAMED RESIDUE: nothing here consults
/// revocation state, and the header says so.
/// ct: public data throughout (certificates carry no secret).
pub fn validate_chain(chain: List[int], hostname: str, anchor: List[int], now: int) -> Cert ! {
    der,
    unsupported_alg,
    bad_signature,
    expired,
    not_yet_valid,
    name_mismatch,
    not_ca,
    issuer_mismatch,
    unknown_critical,
} {
    if chain.len == 0 {
        return der
    }
    let anchor_cert = parse(anchor)?
    check_cert_at(anchor_cert, now)?
    let leaf_stop = cert_end(chain, 0)?
    let leaf = parse(sub(chain, 0, leaf_stop))?
    check_cert_at(leaf, now)?
    check_hostname(leaf, hostname)?
    var child = parse(sub(chain, 0, leaf_stop))?
    var pos = leaf_stop
    var below = 0
    while pos < chain.len {
        let nxt = cert_end(chain, pos)?
        let parent = parse(sub(chain, pos, nxt))?
        check_cert_at(parent, now)?
        if !bytes_eq(child.issuer, parent.subject) {
            return issuer_mismatch
        }
        check_issuer_at(parent, below)?
        verify_signed_by(child, parent)?
        child = parent
        below = below + 1
        pos = nxt
    }
    if !bytes_eq(child.issuer, anchor_cert.subject) {
        return issuer_mismatch
    }
    check_issuer_at(anchor_cert, below)?
    verify_signed_by(child, anchor_cert)?
    leaf
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Hex rendering — this module's own (the ladder precedent, so tests
// and docs can import exactly one module). NOT constant-time; a
// debug/vector convenience only.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Lowercase hex of a byte list. ct: public data (and a rendering
/// convenience only, never a secret-handling path).
pub fn to_hex(b: List[int]) -> str {
    let alphabet = "0123456789abcdef"
    var out = ""
    for v in b {
        require_byte(v)
        let hi = v / 16
        let lo = v % 16
        let hd = alphabet[hi..hi + 1]
        let ld = alphabet[lo..lo + 1]
        out = "{out}{hd}{ld}"
    }
    out
}
